{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 68c0f42fee192e24bc194ec262cad359e4784fd3, artifact 4eb2dc81fd837b4cd7e3ad5b8aeaf21e096ff12e. One condition is deliberately unmet and I state it first.\n\nThe end-to-end dogfood on a real gitseq issue has not happened. It opens a pull request on a public repository, which I cannot take back by superseding an event, and it needs an outbound charter — the one that existed was empty-bodied and premised on a retired inbound charter, so I withdrew it rather than leave it to be ratified into a doorstep the connector refuses. That is the order I argued for in the promise: build the half, let its shape settle what the charter should authorize, then have an operator state and ratify a scoped one. I am reporting the condition unmet rather than satisfying it with a dry run and calling it end to end.\n\nWhat is done. A pull request is a rendering rather than a decision. Its body names the governing request, the artifact and the exact head under review, and says plainly that approval and merge happen in gitseq, because a pull request looks exactly like the place a decision is made and here it is not. It carries identifiers rather than a summary: a summary would be a second copy of the record that can disagree with the first, and the reader on GitHub needs to be able to find the original.\n\nOpening one is a command, not a projection. A pull request cannot be idempotently overwritten and a second call makes a second one, so it is invoked deliberately and prints the created number and URL for a report to carry as evidence. It fails when GitHub refuses, and also when GitHub accepts and returns nothing usable — that second case matters because it would otherwise leave a promise looking kept with no evidence able to close it.\n\nThe read-back defence arrives with the fetch that makes it reachable, which is why deleting it from the inbound half was right rather than merely tidy. The test the conditions asked for renders a proposal, feeds the result back through the inbound half exactly as GitHub would return it, and shows no act is produced. That rendering is excluded twice over, as a pull request and as our own writing, and the ownership test also pins the inverse: an ordinary body is not mistaken for ours, leading whitespace does not defeat the check, and quoting the marker mid-sentence does not counterfeit it.\n\nI want to be plain about what the marker is not. It bounds a loop between two halves of one connector. Any GitHub user can type those characters, so it decides nothing about what a stranger may say, and the comment in the code says so rather than leaving a reader to assume a security boundary.\n\nEvery proposal field is required and none inferred. A rendering naming the wrong head, or no durable request, points somewhere other than the record it claims to render, and the reader has no way to tell — refusing costs one message, guessing costs trust in every rendering. Verified against the live workroom: the retired charter is refused with \"charter is retired\", and an incomplete proposal with \"--commit is required with --propose\". Writing runs behind the same doorstep as reading.\n\nGates: `go test ./internal/connector/... ./cmd/gitseq-github/ -count=1` passes and `go vet ./...` is clean. The worktree is clean at the reported head, which is cut from current main and carries the merged inbound half.","body":{"head":"68c0f42fee192e24bc194ec262cad359e4784fd3","status":"ready-for-review"}}